  "strategy": "We approach the problem using a probabilistically-inspired optimization that minimizes the maximum autocorrelation peak by maintaining a balance between smoothness and sufficient amplitude. The key ideas are:\n\n1. Structure and rationale\n   Represent candidate sequences as samples drawn from a continuous probability distribution, normalized to have a sum of 1. The sequence length is varied between 200 and 800. The objective function is minimized by avoiding overly peaked autocorrelations, achieved through controlled randomness in the time domain that ensures signals decay smoothly. We use a combination of spectrum flattening in Fourier space and gradient projection to update the distribution parameters.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Variables: Coefficient array `a` of length n, with sum(a)=1. Normalized to [0,1000].\n   - Objective: 2 *\n the real definition.\n\n Each sequence a must satisfy:\n   - Non-negative elements (handled by normalize and clipping)\n   - Sum >= 0.01 (fixed to 1 for optimization)\n   - At least 200 coefficients to avoid local peaks\n\n The optimization alternates between:\n   - Soft constraint in frequency domain: Keep power spectrum as flat as possible\n   - L2 projection in time domain: penalize large peak coefficients\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   def propose_candidate(budget_s=1500):\n        start_time = time.time()\n        deadline = start_time + budget_s - 5  # Reserve 5 seconds for final steps\n\n        # Load parent sequence if available\n        parent, parent_obj = try_load_parent()\n        if parent is None:\n            n_best = 200  # Start with minimum sequence length\n        else:\n            n_best = len(parent)\n            parent = np.clip(parent, 0, 1000)\n\n        # Initialize sequence\n        base_sum = 1.0 if n_best==0 else sum(parent)\n        sequence = np.ones(n_best)/n_best if n_best>0 else np.linspace(0.0, 1000.0, base_sum)\n\n        # Ensure finite floating-point numbers\n        if any([np.isnan(x) for x in sequence]):\n            sequence = np.clip(sequence,0.0,1000.0)\n\n        # Set up candidate space with time-energy trade-off\n        candidates = []  # Store (sequence, obj) pairs for comparison\n\n        # Primary optimization phase (until time limit)\n        n_iterations = 8  # Designed to keep iterations within Python's JIT limits\n        for iteration in range(n_iterations):\n            if time.time()-start_time>deadline-1: break  # Check remaining time\n            elapsed = time.time()-start_time\n            time_per_candidate = 2.0 - 0.00005*time_per_candidate  # Prevent memory bloat\n\n            # Sample candidate by varying distribution parameters\n            if iteration < 4:  # Early exploration\n                uniform_candidate = np.ones(n_best)/n_best\n                uniform_obj = evaluate_sequence(uniform_candidate)\n                candidates.append((uniform_candidate, uniform_obj))\n\n                # Gaussian candidate with variance based on iteration\n                a_gauss = np.random.normal(0.5, 0.2, n_best)\n                a_gauss = normalize(a_gauss, method='energy')\n                gaussian_obj = evaluate_sequence(a_gauss)\n                candidates.append((a_gauss, gaussian_obj))\n\n            elif iteration < 6:  # Intensive exploration\n                # Build Toeplitz from candidate to capture autocorrelation\n                a_candidate = normalize(sequence)\n                A = build_toeplitz(a_candidate)\n                C = -np.ones(n_best)  # L1 penalty direction\n                # Solve LP: maximize sum(g) subject to ||f*g||inf <= M\n                solver = linprog(c=C, A_ub=A, b_ub=[np.inf]*time_per_candidate, method='highs')\n\n                if solver is not None:\n                    gaussian_seq = solver.x\n                    gaussian_obj = evaluate_sequence(gaussian_seq)\n                    candidates.append((gaussian_seq, gaussian_obj))\n\n            else:  # Final polishing\n                # Use conjugate gradient to refine widest candidate within time\n                current_obj = evaluate_sequence(sequence)\n                for refine in range(3):\n                    # Compute gradient components\n                    conv_val = np.convolve(sequence, sequence, mode='full')\n                    max_conv = np.max(conv_val)\n                    grad_norm = max_conv / current_obj\n\n                    # Distribute gradient with soft-max weighting\n                    grad = np.random.rand(n_best) + grad_norm  # Build approximation\n                    grad = grad / np.sum(grad)  # Normalize\n                    grad_flattened = evaluate_sequence(grad)  # Real scoring\n\n                    # Update by gradient descent with cooling schedule\n                    sequence -= 0.01 * grad_flattened\n                    new_obj = evaluate_sequence(sequence)\n\n                    if new_obj < current_obj - 1e-6:\n                        current_obj = new_obj\n                    else:\n                        break  # Failed improvement\n\n                # Final candidate update\n                weighted = 0.8 * sequence + 0.2 * np.random.rand(n_best)\n                weighted = normalize(weighted)\n                final_obj_candidate = evaluate_sequence(weighted)\n\n        # Compare all candidates and update sequence\n        best_sequence, best_object = None, np.inf\n        for seq, obj in candidates:\n            if obj < best_object:\n                best_object = obj\n                best_sequence = seq\n\n        # Update global variable\n        height_sequence_1 = best_sequence  \n        return best_sequence.tolist()\nEpoch limitation: 36 total per distribution, but we prioritize floating-point candidates.\n\n4. Search choices\n   - Primary sequence must maintain sum=1\n   - Use iterative updates: starting with uniform, then Gaussian, then conjugate gradient polishing\n   - Clip values to [0,1000] after LBFGS update\n   - Report on expected improvement for multi-run testing\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Total iterations: 12 ~ 1,080 (optional 36 tests per run) but we limit to 6.\n   Continuity check: all coefficients are [0,1000]\n   Time: maximum 1500 seconds / 18, so about 83 seconds per candidate.\n\n6. Validation and return\n   - Final sequence must be normalized [0,1000]\n   - Scoring: 2*n*max(convolve(a))/ (sum(a))^2\n   - Minimized score within 1502 seconds compared to stable LP variants.\n\nThe above strategy combines probabilistic distribution learning with floating-point sequence optimization to avoid the combinatorial bottleneck while gradually refining candidates.",
